From 8d4ad8fce2d3ffb5be868dfe38f76daa5877c519 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@lccc.local>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:21:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] i686: restore global store->load forwarding as a CFG
 pass; fix a narrow-store miscompile

S17 deleted `global_store_forwarding` because it was unsound; S18 replaced it
with the x87 GP-pair folds and a windowed `forward_slot_loads`.  This restores
the capability the deleted pass was for -- cross-basic-block, loop-carried and
cross-call frame-slot forwarding -- with a design that is sound by
construction, and fixes a silent miscompile the new differential fuzzer found
in the windowed pass that is still on main.

1. CFG-wide slot forwarding (`forward_slot_loads_cfg`, new)

   The four defects that made the deleted pass unsound are each closed by
   construction and pinned by a test:

     a. byte-range invalidation was wrong  -> one shared `frame_write_extents`
        / `frame_kill_extents` core, width-exact and mechanically verified
        against all 47 frame-operand shapes the corpus emits (this is how
        `fstenv`/`fnstenv` -- 28-byte WRITES that no hand-written mnemonic
        table listed -- were caught);
     b. the write check only saw `(%ebp)`  -> both `%esp`- and `%ebp`-relative
        slots are tracked, `%esp` keys renumbered on every stack move;
     c. `-off-1` vs `ESP_SLOT_BIAS` rejected all 100,442 `%esp` operands -> the
        bias is now the single source of truth;
     d. `%esp` movement was never fenced   -> push/pop/call/`ret $N` (callee
        pop RAISES %esp, so displacements SHRINK: `shift_esp(-pop)`) renumber
        biased keys only, testing the base before shifting; `%ebp` slots never
        move.

   Dataflow: per-slot value + 8 register stamps, meet = intersection at joins,
   `REG_DEF_ENTRY` (not CONFLICT) at function entry -- a forward only has to
   prove "not redefined since the store", which is independent of provenance;
   CONFLICT is meaningful only at a join where predecessors disagree.  Labels
   head their block; a block leader is the label line itself, not the line
   after it.  Metadata directives (`.cfi_*`, `.loc`, `.p2align`) do not kill
   state -- they follow every call and prologue, and killing there destroys
   exactly the cross-call forwards the pass exists for; only real data
   directives do.  setjmp/longjmp bails out; escape analysis gates the
   indirect-memory relaxation while indexed frame accesses kill
   unconditionally.  Budget 40k lines, 48 slots.  Runs at the head of phase 4:
   it nops redundant reloads, and a pass that deletes lines other passes
   pattern-match on must run after them (placing it earlier stole anchors from
   downstream folds).  GP `movl/movw/movb` only: `fstpl S; fldl S` is never
   collapsed.  Kill switches `CCC_NO_GLOBAL_SLOT_FWD`, `CCC_NO_SLOT_FWD_PRECISION`.

   The windowed pass keeps its historical point window unchanged; upgrading it
   to the precise model was measured net-negative (worth 417/462 sites in
   theory, -5 instructions corpus-wide and +2 on loop_rotate, because it stole
   the load-op-store anchor `collapse_slot_rmw_i686` needs) and is recorded as
   a closed negative result.

2. Silent miscompile: narrow store forwarded into a full-width ALU operand

   `rewrite_alu_slot_source` matched any 32-bit ALU mnemonic reading the slot
   and rewrote it to the store's source register without checking the STORE
   width, so

       movw %ax, 8(%esp) / addl 8(%esp), %esi  ->  addl %eax, %esi

   substituted a 16-bit value for the 32-bit slot contents.  In C:
   `u.h[0] = acc & 0xffff; acc += u.w;`.  Wrong at -O1/-Os/-O2/-O3, i686 only
   (x86-64's store forwarder rewrites loads and requires exact width, or the
   sound qword->dword case where the stored eight bytes do define the low
   four).  Found by the new differential fuzzer: both lccc arms disagreed with
   gcc, which is what showed it predates this pass.  Fixed by making the width
   a parameter no caller can forget (`store_size != MoveSize::L` refuses), with
   a unit pin proven to fail without the guard and a runtime regression test.
   A narrow store could be merged with an older full-width store of the same
   slot to recover the wide value, but that needs a per-byte slot model; the
   refusal costs nothing measurable (below).

3. `scripts/godbolt.py`: ICC labels its entry block `<function>.:`, which the
   body scanner treated as a new symbol, truncating `word_pun` to one
   instruction and printing icc=1 against clang=9 -- a bogus "optimal" oracle
   row, the exact failure mode `_label_is_function` exists to prevent.

Evidence (i686, 809-TU tests/ corpus, fastbuild):

  base  270,945 insns / 102,007 slot refs
  both  270,698 insns / 101,602 slot refs   -247 insns (-0.091%), -405 refs
        108 TUs improved, 0 TUs regressed
  soundness fix alone (base vs both kill switches off):
        +0 insns, +1 slot ref, 0 TUs changed -- free

  runtime: lz4_compress and 7 further benchmarks are hash-IDENTICAL, so the
  binding no-regression constraint holds by construction; on the 8 benchmarks
  whose assembly changed, paired alternating timing gives geomean min +0.47% /
  median +0.09% / paired -0.23% -- neutral, and reported as such rather than
  as a win.
  fuzzer: 624 differential cases over 12 generators (punning, dynamic index,
  cross-block, loop-carried, across-call, %esp movement, volatile, setjmp,
  x87 overlap, escape, wide/narrow, struct fields); final two seeds 480/480
  agree with gcc and with each other.
  tests: cargo test 2,544 passed / 0 failed (946 in peephole, +32 new);
  cargo fmt and clippy --all-targets clean.

Honest verdict: this is a code-size and memory-traffic improvement, not a
benchmark win.  Frame-slot forwarding is now ~93% exhausted (431 of 462
modelled sites).  The remaining ~33k GP slot loads are a location-allocation
problem, not a forwarding problem -- the oracle comparison in
results/godbolt_narrow_store makes that concrete: on `word_pun` lccc emits 30
instructions against gcc 15 / icc 19 / clang 9 / icx 9 because it homes the
union in a stack slot while the oracles keep it in registers.
